import types

VERSION = "{{OK_PLANNER_VERSION}}"
RESULT_LINE_BYTES = 512

USAGE = """tests — the mechanical steps of a sprint's tests: the offers, the
scenario list, the runs, the plan, the tasks a sprint's test stages
file, and the stamp.

  tests offers [--json]
      The groups of test work /plan-sprint offers at Frame, each with
      its key, of the kinds testing.offers lists: stale stories, each
      story whose file's blob id differs from its test stamp, and each
      story with recorded scenarios and no stamp; untested stories, each
      story with no recorded scenario, no test stamp, and no unguarded
      answer naming it; legacy folders, the test files no scenario
      claims, one group per folder. No offer names the runtime parts:
      tests groups plans them with no offer. Stale and untested stories
      group as tests groups groups stories: stories one file cites join one group of up
      to testing.group_size, split in catalog order, and the rest are
      batched by testing.group_size in catalog order. It refuses unless
      testing.enabled is on.
  tests scenarios [--json]
      The scenario list as it stands, each story's test stamp first,
      then the lock list, then each scenario with its locks.
  tests locks [--json]
  tests locks add <name> --guards "<the shared thing it guards>"
  tests locks remove <name>
      The lock list, the names a test may declare a lock on, each with
      the one line saying what shared thing it guards. add adds a name,
      and refuses one that is not a slug, one already listed, and an
      empty or multi-line --guards. remove removes a name, and refuses
      one the list does not hold, and one a scenario of the scenario
      list, an entry of test_support_locks, or a scenario of a
      -plan.json beside a sprint under .ok-planner/sprints/ still
      declares. Each write takes the scenario list's lock, reads the
      list inside it, replaces the file atomically, and prints the name
      it added or removed. --json goes with the listing alone.
  tests convert-state [--clear <story slug> ...] [--dry-run]
      Convert the scenario list, and each -plan.json beside a sprint
      under .ok-planner/sprints/, from an earlier form to the form this
      tool reads. It reads no testing config. The first line it prints
      says the scenario list's form: none: where the file does not
      exist, current: where it holds no earlier form, and earlier:
      where it holds one or more, naming each; only an earlier form
      converts. The first earlier form holds one top-level stamp for
      the whole list:
      each story with recorded scenarios gets as its test stamp the git
      blob id of .ok-planner/design/stories/<slug>.md at the stamp's
      commit, and none where that commit is not in the repository, the
      file is absent at it, or --clear names the story, and the
      conversion drops the top-level stamp and writes ranking,
      acceptances, and reaches empty. The second records a scenario's
      test support files under support: the conversion moves them to
      test_support, and, where the list holds no top-level stamp,
      drops the test stamp of each story --clear names. The third
      declares a scenario's shared resources under resources and reads
      in place of locks, or holds no lock_list: the conversion makes
      each resources name a lock held exclusive, drops each reads name
      and the implied read of every resource with no shared lock in
      their place, adds each converted name to lock_list with the line
      "converted from the shared resource <name>; say what shared
      thing it guards", writes test_support_locks empty where absent,
      and drops the budget's preflight mark, so the next tested
      sprint's preflight checks the suite command against the locks.
      Each plan converts the same way, one line each, <plan>:
      converts: <what changes>: each scenario's resources become
      exclusive locks and its reads go; each scenario whose latest
      tests item, in the sprint's run and then its certification
      ledger, records a resources list and no locks takes that list as
      its exclusive locks; and its budget's preflight mark goes where
      the plan converts or the scenario list converts the third form.
      Where either converts, each preflight task of the sprint's run
      that closed done passed before the locks: the plan sets it aside
      under preflights_set_aside, so the sprint's next write stage files
      a preflight that checks the suite command against the locks, and
      the sprint's stamp records no preflight mark from it.
      Each name a converted plan holds and the lock list does not joins
      the list, which the tool writes, a new one where none exists. The
      conversion keeps kept and every other field. --dry-run prints
      one JSON object on standard output, {"<project-relative path>":
      "<the text the write would leave>", ...}, one entry per file it
      would rewrite and {} where none converts, its other lines on
      standard error, and writes nothing; otherwise it replaces the
      scenario list under its lock and then each plan, each file
      atomically, and prints converted: <path> for each.
  tests declare [--enabled on|frozen|off] [--run <command>] [--silence <seconds>]
                [--named yes|no] [--leftovers <command>] [--slowest <n>]
                [--chunk <n>] [--group-size <n>] [--runners [<command> ...]]
                [--setup <command> | none]
                [--setup-silence <seconds>]
                [--reaches <files | folders | none> --reaches-said <the owner's words>]
                [--offers [untested | stale | legacy ...]]
      Write the keys given into testing in .ok-planner/config.json, keep
      the others, and drop the earlier testing.timeout and
      testing.schedule. /ok-setup is the verb that runs it. enabled is
      on, frozen, or off; under off, groups, plan (but plan --show and plan --questions), reached,
      slowest, reports, settled, tasks --stage speed, and run --sprint
      refuse, and every other verb, run without --sprint among them,
      still runs. offers lists the standing offers /plan-sprint
      makes, each kind at most once, which enabled on needs; it refuses
      runtime, since runtime parts are planned with no offer. run is the
      suite command, holding {tests} and {results}; silence, the whole seconds
      with no result after which a run stops; named, whether the suite
      command runs a test by its name inside its file; leftovers, a
      command that prints one JSON object counting each kind of resource
      a test may leave behind, {} where it counts none; slowest, how
      many of the slowest tests each run lists; chunk, the most
      scenarios of one story one test task takes; group_size, the most
      stories one planning group or offer group holds; runners, the project's
      own test-running commands, which review checks refuses as a check,
      none where it names no runner of its own; setup, a command each
      run runs before the suite command to get the test environment
      ready, safe to run again and fast where the environment is ready,
      or none, written null, where the project needs no setup;
      setup_silence, the whole seconds with no output after which setup
      stops, which a declared setup needs; and
      reaches, {"form", "said"}, records what
      the suite command writes as each test's reaches and the owner's
      words for it: files, where each line names the product files the
      test ran; folders, where it names the folders of the services the
      test reached; or none, where it writes no reaches. --reaches and
      --reaches-said go together. While said is null, the tool uses the
      form the key names. --timeout is refused: a run stops after
      testing.silence seconds with no result or output instead.
      --schedule and --schedule-said are refused: each round runs under
      the locks its tests declare (tests locks), and the tool checks
      each round against them, so no schedule is recorded.
  tests enabled
      Print testing.enabled: on, frozen, or off. It needs no other
      testing key, and refuses where testing.enabled is missing or holds
      any other value.
  tests profiles
      Check the lean profiles' copies of the cheatsheets against
      .ok-planner/lean-profiles.json, the record true-up writes with
      each lean profile: the hash of each cheatsheet under
      .claude/rules/ whose sections the profile's body copies. It
      prints lean profiles current where each recorded cheatsheet
      stands and matches its hash. It refuses, naming each cheatsheet
      that differs or is gone and saying to run /ok, where one does,
      and where the record is missing or unreadable. tasks runs the
      same check first for --stage write, verify, and speed, and files
      nothing while it fails.
  tests budget --from-run <run>
      Write the test stack's budget into the scenario list from the
      latest done budget task of the run /ok-setup opened, with its
      limits, refusing as tests plan refuses a budget that does not
      hold, and print it.
  tests machine [--sprint <path>] [--workers <n> --reason <text> --evidence <text> | --clear --reason <text>] [--json]
      Measure this machine: its cores, its memory, its Docker memory,
      and each running container with the memory it holds, and their
      sum, printing one line per measurement as it takes it, to stderr
      under --json. Docker is asked through docker-sweep, each query
      bounded by stack_helpers.docker_query_seconds; where docker is not
      on PATH or does not answer, the Docker fields are null with the
      reason. With no --workers or --clear it writes nothing and prints
      the measurement beside the record at
      .ok-planner/.cache/tests-machine.json, outside the committed tree:
      this machine's count, the count a run here uses and where it comes
      from, and the record's history. --workers <n> --reason
      "<constraint>" --evidence "<a round, a preflight task, or a
      measurement>" writes the record: the measurement taken then, the
      count, and a dated history entry with the reason, the evidence,
      and the measurement, appended to the entries before it. --clear
      --reason "<text>" sets the count to none and appends an entry, so
      runs go back to the budget's workers. The three of --workers go
      together, --clear takes --reason alone, and each text must be
      non-empty. --workers refuses a count below 1 or above the budget's
      workers: the plan's of the sprint --sprint names, the scenario
      list's where it has no plan or without --sprint, and one where no
      budget stands. The write takes .ok-planner/.cache/tests-machine.lock,
      reads the history inside it, and replaces the record atomically; a
      record that is no machine record is replaced, its history started
      afresh, with a line saying so. --json prints the measurement and
      the record as JSON, or the record a write wrote.
  tests run [--sprint <path> [--failing | --changed]] [--only <scenario> ...] [--from <file>] [--seed <n>]
            [--workers <n>] [--json]
      Run the tests in one run of testing.run, the project's suite
      command. {tests} becomes the path of a JSON file, {"workers": <n>,
      "seed": <n>, "tests": [{"path", "name", "locks": [{"name",
      "mode"}]}]}, the run using at most those workers, each test's
      locks its own and those of its test support, and {results} the
      path of a stream the command writes one line to per test as each
      finishes: {"path", "name", "passed": true or false, "seconds":
      <number>, "output": "<text>", "reaches": [<path>, ...], "started":
      <number>, "ended": <number>}, reaches optional: the paths inside
      the project the test reached, each ending in / a folder that
      covers every file under it. A reaches that is not a list of
      non-empty paths inside the project is dropped with a note, and the
      result stands. started and ended, optional together: the test's
      start and end in seconds on the suite command's own clock. A line
      with one of them alone, a value that is no number, or ended before
      started keeps its result, and its times are dropped with a note.
      The command writes its lines to that path and never replaces the
      file. Several workers may write it at
      once, so each line, newline included, stays within %d bytes, the
      least PIPE_BUF POSIX allows: cap output to fit. A test's first
      line stands; a later line, or one that is not JSON or lacks path,
      passed, or seconds, is unmatched; a test with no line fails where
      the round ran to its end, and is not run where the run stopped or
      the round did not close, which no failure reader counts as its
      own failure: tests failures lists a stop that left a test with no
      result as one stopped--silence entry. The run records each line's arrival, its seconds since the suite
      command started, and each line's started and ended where it
      carries both.
      Where testing.setup is a command, the run runs it first, with no
      clock but testing.setup_silence seconds with no output, and starts
      the suite command only after setup exits 0; a failed setup fails
      every test with a tail that opens setup failed. The run stops
      after testing.silence seconds with no result line and no output,
      or, while a test that records a silence of its own has no result,
      after the longest such silence. Without
      --sprint, the tests the stamp recorded; with --sprint, the tests
      the sprint plans, each test with its locks and the locks of its
      test support. The run refuses a test that declares a lock name
      the lock list does not hold, or a mode other than exclusive and
      shared, naming the test, the name, and tests locks add. A --from
      entry may carry locks, a list of {name, mode}.
      Each run draws a new order seed, a whole number from 0 to
      4294967295, and lists the tests in the order of each test's key
      under it: the first 16 hex digits of the sha256 of
      "<seed>:<scenario id>", or of the locator for a --from entry with
      no scenario. --seed <n> takes the order seed a round's header
      records, so a run of any subset of that round's tests keeps their
      order in it. The suite command starts the tests in the listed
      order, and starts a test only once it holds every lock the test
      declares: two tests that hold one lock name, at least one of them
      exclusive, never run at the same time.
      A run uses this machine's count where
      .ok-planner/.cache/tests-machine.json records one, never more than
      the budget's workers, and the budget's workers otherwise: the
      plan's with --sprint and the stamp's without, one where it records
      none. --workers <n> runs that count in place of both, so a fixer
      reproduces a round at the count it ran, and refuses a count below
      1 or above the budget's workers. A record that is no machine
      record is refused, naming tests machine --workers and --clear,
      which write it again. Before setup starts, each run prints one
      line to stderr, run: seed <n>, <w> workers from <source>, the
      source --workers, this machine's record, or the budget. Every run
      that names --sprint records a round in the sprint's -results.jsonl, of
      kind whole where it runs the whole suite and of kind rerun
      otherwise: it prints each result as it lands, and appends the whole
      round, its header, its results, and its close, once at its end,
      under a short lock, inside which the round takes its number. The
      header records the order seed, the workers and where their count
      came from, workers_from: flag, record, or budget, each planned
      test with its locks, the git blob of each test file in the plan's
      folders and of each test support file its tests name, and each task closed by then in the sprint's run and,
      as <ledger>#<task>, in its certification ledger. A run
      that fails partway still appends the results it has, its close
      recording the error, then fails. Around a recorded round,
      testing.leftovers runs before and after, and the round records
      both counts and each kind whose count grew, which fails the round.
      A whole-suite round refuses at once while another whole-suite
      round of the same sprint is under way, naming that run and the
      results file. Before testing.setup and the suite command start, a
      run takes each lock name its tests declare, in name order, and
      holds it until the run ends: exclusive where one of its tests
      holds the name exclusive, shared otherwise. A run of isolated
      tests takes no lock. A run that must wait for a lock another run
      holds prints one line naming that run and the lock, then waits
      for it. Each live test's standing result is
      its latest result from the latest closed whole-suite round onward,
      that round included, with the blobs its round's header recorded.
      --failing reruns each live test whose standing result failed and
      no hold settles, as tests failures says, never a not run test; it refuses
      where no round is recorded or no such test stands, which sends you
      to the whole suite as the gate. --changed reruns each live test
      whose test file or test support files differ from the blobs of its
      standing result, or that has no standing result or one not run,
      and refuses where none does, naming the whole suite. --only,
      --from, --failing, and --changed each choose the tests, so a run
      takes at most one; --failing and --changed need --sprint.
      --only prints the testing.leftovers counts around its run,
      recorded or not; --from runs the tests a JSON list of {path,
      name, scenario} names, takes no --sprint, and records no round.
      It prints each result, then the
      testing.slowest slowest tests by what each costs the round's wall
      time under the tests' locks: the tool takes a lower bound on the
      round's wall time, and a test's cost is how much the bound drops
      when its seconds drop to zero. The bound is the larger of all
      tests' seconds over the workers and, for each lock name, its
      exclusive holders' seconds summed plus the larger of its longest
      shared holder's seconds and its shared holders' total over the
      workers; a test that holds no lock adds only to the first. Each
      test's locks are its own and those of its test support. Every
      slowest list this tool prints or reads is ranked this way, a
      round's from the workers and locks its header records, or, for a
      round an earlier release recorded without them, the plan's budget
      workers and the locks each live test declares.
  tests reached --sprint <path> --files <path> ...
      The scenario ids a fix that changed the files given reruns, one
      per line. The map of each test's reaches is the scenario list's,
      overlaid by the sprint's rounds in order: each run of a test
      replaces its reaches, and a run that carries none leaves the test
      with no record; a not run result changes nothing. Where
      testing.reaches names files or folders, it prints each live test
      whose reaches names a file given or a folder above one, and each
      live test with no reaches; where no live test's reaches names a
      file given, every live test. Where testing.reaches is none, it
      prints none and says to rerun only the tests the fix was for,
      and that the fix prompt counts a failing test's pass alone as
      fixed where any fix landed since its failing round. Run them
      with tests run --sprint <path> --only.
  tests groups --sprint <path> [--replan <story slug> ...] [--take <offer key> ...]
               [--new <story slug> ...] [--widen [<story slug> ...]]
               [--parts <part slug> ...]
               [--unguarded <path>=<the owner's reason> ...] [--frozen] [--json]
      Write the sprint's test scope beside the sprint, -groups.json in
      place of .md, and print each group's key, its stories with the
      reasons each is touched, and its brief, and the inputs every
      planner's brief carries. It reads no planning run: /plan-sprint
      runs it to set the scope, and the sprint's test planning step
      plans inside it. --replan touches each tested story whose
      behavior the sprint changes, with the reason behavior. --take
      takes each offer group `tests offers` prints by its key: a stale
      or untested group touches each of its stories with the reason
      stale or untested, and a legacy group puts its folder's test
      files in the sort; --take runtime is refused, since runtime parts
      are planned with no offer. --parts names each runtime part the
      sprint's work items add, change, or remove, by the slug a runtime
      scenario's part field and id use. Under on, the runtime planner
      runs in every sprint, for those parts and for every part with no
      recorded scenario; under frozen, it runs only where --parts names
      a part, for those parts' rewrites and retirements, and every
      other recorded runtime scenario stands as recorded. The record
      carries the parts, and the verb prints the runtime planner's
      brief line. --new
      touches each story the sprint's deltas add, with the reason new,
      and refuses a slug no ### New story: <slug> delta of the sprint
      names. --widen touches each story it names, with the reason
      widen; with no slug, each story whose earlier calls, as `tests
      calls` lists them, left a path out. --unguarded records each
      program a ruled issue leaves unguarded, with the owner's reason:
      the inputs line names each one, and the plan carries each into
      the scenario list. --frozen marks every group's brief frozen, so
      its planner plans no new scenario; it is given exactly where
      testing.enabled is frozen, and takes no --take, --new, or
      --widen. It lists the test files to sort, those of the taken
      legacy folders, and as kept, left as they stand, every other test
      file no scenario claims. It prints nothing to plan where no story
      is touched, no legacy folder is taken, the runtime planner does
      not run, and no program is left unguarded. Stories one file cites join one group
      of up to testing.group_size stories, split in catalog order; the
      other touched stories are batched by that size in catalog order.
      It refuses a --replan or --widen slug with no story file, an
      offer key `tests offers` does not print, a --parts value that is
      not a slug, and an --unguarded value that is not <path>=<reason>
      or names a path twice.
  tests calls <story slug> ... [--json]
      For each story, the plan of the closed sprint, or of an earlier
      release's test sprint, that planned it last, read from
      .ok-planner/test-plans/ for each sprint the closed-record ledger
      lists with a commit, newest first by those commits, and that
      plan's calls
      for the story that left a path out: each call of kind untested,
      or every call of the story in a plan whose calls carry no kind.
      A plan that records no planned stories planned every story. A
      call belongs to a story by its story field, else by a site or
      key that is the story's slug or starts with <slug>--.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> [--frozen]
      Assemble the sprint's plan from the planners' items and the
      groups `tests groups` wrote, check it, and write it beside the
      sprint. It takes each story's
      items from the latest done story task that records a stories item
      for it, keeps each recorded scenario of a story no group lists,
      and of a story whose every reason is widen each recorded
      scenario its planner names nowhere, and refuses a touched story
      no done story task records, and a run with no groups. It asks
      the owner nothing: it refuses a question on a scenario, naming its
      task, since a scenario whose outcome the sprint changes is a
      rewrite and one whose code the sprint removes is a retire with
      gap true, each with a call; a retire with gap true records the
      scenario as a gap at the stamp. It records each program question,
      an entry point no story serves, in the plan's questions, which
      sprint certification's owner list files in the intake, and
      carries into the plan's unguarded list the scenario list's
      unguarded programs and those the groups record. It refuses each
      new or rewritten scenario with no entry, kind, or setup, with an
      entry left unguarded, or with an entry a program question names. --frozen is given exactly
      where testing.enabled is frozen, and refuses each new scenario.
      It refuses a legacy entry whose path lies outside the legacy
      folders the groups took, and a test file to sort that no
      scenario claims and the sort names nowhere, and records as kept
      every test file the groups keep that no planned scenario claims:
      each unclaimed test file outside the taken folders. It takes the
      budget and its limits from the latest done budget task, and
      refuses one that records no budget item or more than one, workers
      that is not a whole number of 1 or more, a budget that names no
      stack or no sizing, and a limit handled other than by raise,
      split, or lease. Where no budget task closed done, the plan
      carries the budget the scenario list records, its preflight mark
      included, and checks none. It carries the preflights_set_aside
      list of the plan it replaces. It refuses a scenarios item that
      records resources or reads, naming its task to refile, a
      scenario's locks that is not a list of {name, mode}, each name a
      slug, each mode exclusive or shared, no name twice, and a lock
      name the lock list does not hold. It takes the
      time controls from the controls items, one per id, and refuses
      two that record one id with different fields, a new or rewritten
      scenario with no waits or one that is none of event: <what the
      product emits>, control: <a time control>, and clock: <the clock
      the test moves>, a new or rewritten scenario's waits that names a
      control--<slug> no item records, and a time control whose form is not trigger, clock, or
      setting, that names no site or no files, that names no gate where
      it is a trigger or a clock or one where it is a setting, or that
      no scenario waits on; each becomes a work item of the sprint, which
      a code stage builds. It keeps each fixture a kept scenario's test
      uses as that test's test support, keeps each fixture a test file
      it records as kept reads, records that fixture as kept too, and
      deletes each one no kept test uses. It refuses a deletion or port
      of a file a kept scenario names as test support or a kept test
      file reads, a test support entry that names no test that reads
      it, a
      port of rule inferred that states no inferred rule, and inferred
      text beside any other sort or rule.
      Where testing.named is false, an adopted scenario's test is its
      whole file.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --from-run <planning run> --questions
      The program questions the planning run's programs task raised,
      from the planner tasks whose items the assembly takes, as JSON:
      each entry point no story serves, which every later planner's
      brief names.
  tests plan --sprint <path> --show [--json]
      Print the plan: a summary first (scenarios per story by action,
      the untested and departure calls whole, the other calls by kind,
      each inferred rule a port enforces with its test and check, the
      planned tests by kind and setup, each lock name with each test
      that holds it and its mode, the budget, each time control the plan builds, and each
      program question), then the counts and the listing. --json prints
      the plan file as it stands, with questions empty where a plan an
      earlier release wrote records none.
  tests tasks --sprint <path> --stage <write | sweep | verify | speed> [--run <run file>]
              [--after <task> ...] [--from state | --round <n>]
      File the sprint's test tasks for one stage into a task run: the
      run file --run names, which must exist, or else the sprint's
      -run.jsonl. Every stage first refuses, filing nothing, a lock name
      the lock list does not hold, or a mode other than exclusive and
      shared, that a scenario of the plan, a live test, or a test
      support file they list declares. Test support, test, verify, and
      speed tasks go to the
      ok-test profile, and preflight, legacy, and sweep tasks to
      ok-opus; a refile keeps its source's profile. The write, verify,
      and speed stages first run the check tests profiles runs, and
      refuse, filing nothing, while it fails. --after, which only the write stage takes, names the
      build tasks of the code stages the test stages follow: every task
      the write stage files new, the preflight included, waits on each
      of them, and the stage refuses, filing nothing, where one names no
      task of the run or closed other than done. On a run other than the
      sprint's -run.jsonl, such as the certification ledger, the write
      stage files only what that run's own items ask for: its sent-back
      tests, its blocked test tasks, and its open test support items; it
      files no preflight, no planned test, and no legacy task. Where the
      plan's budget names a test stack, the write stage first
      files the preflight task alone, refiles it where it closed short
      of done, refuses where it closed blocked twice, and files the rest
      only once it closed done. It files no preflight where the plan's
      budget carries a preflight mark and equals the budget the
      scenario list records, and a preflight passed that budget on this
      machine, as .ok-planner/.cache/tests-preflight.json, outside the
      committed tree, records: a budget a budget task produced carries
      no mark, and a machine whose own preflight never passed the
      budget records none, so each gets a preflight. A write stage that
      finds the sprint's latest preflight closed done records the budget
      it passed in that file, under the machine record's lock. A preflight
      task the plan sets aside under preflights_set_aside counts for
      neither the skip nor the refile. Each run of the write stage is a
      pass. It files a test support task per test support file the
      written scenarios name, and per test support file an open test
      support item names, then the test tasks, then the legacy tasks.
      It files one test task per story, or per chunk of at most
      testing.chunk of the story's scenarios to write, a runtime
      scenario's part standing for its story: keyed
      test:<story or part>:<n>, numbered within the story, citing each
      scenario it takes, its brief carrying each scenario's brief, and
      its files every test file those scenarios name, so two chunks
      that share a test file run one after the other. A scenario some
      test task cites is filed. Every test task the pass files or refiles, and every reopened
      test task it adds waits to, waits on every test support task the
      pass files. A test support task's brief names the locks the
      scenario list or the latest locks item records for its file, and
      the task records the locks the file's code shows as a locks item
      keyed by the path. A test support item,
      in the test-support pool, names a test support file as its path,
      its scenario, and as its change what a test needs: missing, a
      missing helper; changed, a changed helper; fixture; or seed, a
      seed entry, where testing.setup builds a seed; elsewhere briefed
      as a fixture. It stands open until the test support task marks it
      made. The stage refiles each test task that closed blocked having
      filed a test support item after the test support task that makes
      it, adds that test support task to the waits of a test task
      `tasks retry` reopened and no `next` has issued, where a test
      support item it filed stands open, and files a test task
      again for each scenario whose latest tests item a verifier or a
      speed reviewer sent back, one per chunk of each story's sent-back
      tests, keyed and numbered as above, each scenario with the
      reason, after any task that holds its files.
      A rewrite's files hold each test file and each scenario's test
      support files, the plan's and the tests item's, so it may change
      them, and two rewrites that share a file run one after the other.
      The stage refuses, and files nothing, where a test support item
      records another change, where an open test support item's test
      support task closed done, where the last two filings of one test
      task key closed blocked and neither filed a test support item, and
      where a
      task it would file
      or refile waits on a task that closed other than done, naming that
      task and its outcome. The verify
      stage files one verify task per test task whose recorded tests
      items, each a scenario's latest, record a test and stand open,
      keyed verify:<test task>, with no task before it, its brief naming
      the budget's workers and, per item, the test's seconds in the
      latest round its test task ran it in, the locks the test holds,
      each declaration and plan text the item changes, before and
      after, and each lock it adds with its mode, and one per fixes or promises item that stands open, each
      after the last. On a run other than the sprint's -run.jsonl, where
      verify names the fix verifier, the verify stage files its tasks
      with the test-verify prompt. The speed stage takes exactly one source, as tests
      slowest does: --from state, the ranking the scenario list carries,
      in the build stages, or --round <n>, the failed whole-suite round
      sprint certification names. It files one batch: the
      testing.slowest unsettled tests tests slowest prints for that
      source, one speed task each, keyed speed:<scenario>:<the git blob
      of its test file>, save a test whose latest speed verdict sent it
      back, which settles once its rewrite is accepted. Under --from
      state it refuses where a tests item stands sent back or awaits its
      verifier; under --round <n> it refuses unless round n is a closed
      whole-suite round that lists a failure, and refuses a round whose
      only failures are lock breaches, locks-- entries, or tests whose
      tail opens setup failed, faults of the suite command or its setup
      that no speed rewrite fixes. It refiles a speed task
      that closed short of done or with no verdict, or whose test is
      unsettled again, and refuses where two closed short of done or
      with no verdict. --from and --round go with --stage speed alone.
  tests slowest --sprint <path> (--from state | --round <n>) [--json]
      Print the testing.slowest slowest tests whose speed is unsettled,
      each with its rank, the git blob of its test file, the digest of
      its test support files, its median seconds and the count of runs the
      median spans, and its cost, and write nothing. A test's median is
      the median of its passing results in the closed whole-suite rounds
      whose header records its test file at the blob it has now, and of
      the seconds the scenario list's ranking carries for it at that
      blob. --from state ranks every live test by the carried seconds
      alone, with the budget's workers and the locks each live test
      declares; --round <n> ranks the tests round n ran, a closed
      whole-suite round, by the rounds up to n, with the workers and
      locks its header records. Each cost is what the test's median
      costs the round's wall time under the tests' locks, as tests run
      ranks. Speed verdicts, tests items, and fixes items come from the
      sprint's run and its certification ledger, the ledger's later, and
      from the run --stage speed files into, latest of all. A test is
      settled where its latest speed verdict accepted
      it at the blob its test file has now, with the test support digest it
      has now or a median no higher than the seconds accepted; where its
      latest verdict sent it back and its scenario's latest tests item,
      recorded after that verdict, stands accepted; or where its latest
      verdict found it product-slow and no accepted fixes item of its
      verdict's run names that verdict with perf. A test with no verdict
      in either is
      settled where the acceptance the scenario list carries for it
      holds by the same rule. It refuses neither source and both, and a
      round that is no closed whole-suite round.
  tests ports --sprint <path>
      Add each project check the latest legacy task of each file
      recorded to the review loop's checks. It writes nothing, and
      refuses, where checks is not a list of commands or holds one that
      starts a test runner, and where a recorded command starts a test
      runner, as review checks finds one, naming each such ports item,
      the legacy task that recorded it, and the runner, and saying to
      refile that task with the refusal.
  tests snapshot --sprint <path> --key <fix key> [--after] <file> ...
      Keep each file as it stands before a fixer first edits it, or,
      with --after, every file the before snapshot holds as the fixer
      leaves it when it closes.
  tests diff --sprint <path> --key <fix key>
      The change one fixer made, from its two snapshots.
  tests stray --sprint <path> [--json]
      The test files, and the test support files the stamp or the plan
      records, that no planned test claims as its own or as test
      support,
      save each test file the plan or the stamp records as kept. Paths
      compare in normal form, so ./tests/x.py and tests/x.py name one
      file. A sprint with no -plan.json lists none: it plans no tests,
      so it keeps every test file it leaves alone, and a test file a
      code task adds is caught by review certified's check on staged
      test paths.
  tests failures --sprint <path> [--json]
      Each live test whose standing result failed, since the latest
      closed whole-suite round, and, from each round since then,
      leftovers--<kind> for each kind of leftover whose count grew in
      it, stopped--silence where it stopped after testing.silence and
      left a test with no result, naming those tests, and
      locks--<test>--<test> for each pair of tests that ran at the same
      time while both held one lock name, at least one of the two
      exclusive, by the locks the round's header records for each
      planned test, a lock breach. Two tests ran at the same time where
      each started before the other ended: on the suite command's clock
      where both result lines carry started and ended, and otherwise by
      arrival, each test starting at its line's arrival less its seconds
      and ending at its arrival, so no pair mixes two clocks; the
      entry's tail names each lock the two share, each test's mode, the
      times, and the clock that gave them. It checks every round that
      records arrival times, and reads a round whose header records no
      locks, an earlier release's, as one whose tests hold none. A lock
      breach clears once a later closed round reaches both its tests,
      as a rerun round's failure does. It also lists
      perf:<scenario>:<speed item> for each scenario's latest
      product-slow speed verdict, in the sprint's run or its
      certification ledger, that no accepted fixes item of that run
      names with perf=<speed item> and no verified defect of the ledger
      names as its test, with or without a round. Such a verdict is a
      failure where no closed whole-suite round is recorded, or where
      the latest one lists another failure no hold settles, so another
      round runs anyway; otherwise it is deferred: no fix lands, and
      the owner list files it for the next /plan-sprint. --json prints
      round, failures, and deferred. It leaves out each
      failure a hold settles: an owner issue the plan records, a held
      promise of the sprint's run, a promised defect of the
      certification ledger whose test field names it, and an open issue
      the scenario list records on the scenario.
  tests window --sprint <path> --round <n> --test <scenario> [--json]
      The window of one test in one round, which a fixer reads to trace
      a flaky test, and write nothing. It prints the named test's span
      on each clock it has, whether it passed, and its locks, then each
      test whose run overlapped it, then each test that ended before it
      started, each group ordered by how near the test's end lies to
      the named test's start. Each line gives the test's start, its
      end, and the clock that gave them, compared with the named test's
      on one clock per pair, as tests failures compares: the suite
      command's where both result lines carry started and ended, and
      arrival otherwise. A line marks each lock name the test shares
      with the named test, with the test's mode and the named test's,
      by the locks the round's header records; a round whose header
      records no locks, an earlier release's, marks none and says so.
      The named test's line also gives its effective locks now, and a
      test's line marks each name it now shares with the named test
      where that differs from the round's, by the sprint's live tests,
      a fixer's locks items included, so a verifier reads that both
      tests now hold the name a lock fix added. --json gives these as
      now_locks and now_shares.
      It lists last each test the round planned and recorded no times
      for. A named test the round recorded no times for, such as each
      test a stopped round left with no result, has no start: its
      window lists every test the round ran, the latest arrival first,
      each with its span on one clock, the suite command's where its
      line carries started and ended and arrival otherwise, then every
      other test the round recorded no times for, each line marking the
      lock names it shares with the named test, in the round and now.
      It reads no testing config. --json prints round, test,
      overlapped, before, ran, unreported, untimed, and whether the
      round records locks; ran and unreported hold lines only for a
      named test with no times. It refuses a round the sprint's
      -results.jsonl does not hold and a test the round did not plan,
      naming the round and the file.
  tests reports --sprint <path>
      Add one report to the selected run, which must be a certification
      ledger of the sprint, under key gate and area sprint-suite, for
      each failure of the latest closed whole-suite round (entry T1),
      its stopped--silence entry where it stopped and left a test with
      no result (T1), each leftover count that grew in it (T2), each
      perf: entry tests
      failures lists among its failures, never a deferred one (T3), and
      each locks-- entry of the round, a lock breach (T4),
      each with entry, test, the failure's id, round, the round's
      number, files, the reaches of its tests, and site. A T1 report
      also carries rerun, passed or failed, from the first rerun round
      after it that ran that test alone. It adds no report for a
      failure a hold settles, as tests failures says, nor one a report
      of the run already names, by test and round, or by test alone for
      perf:. It prints JSON: the reports it added, the failures already
      reported, and the failures held.
  tests settled --sprint <path> --ledger <ledger>
      Check the sprint's suite for certification and write nothing. The
      ledger must be the one this tool reads for the sprint. It holds
      where a closed whole-suite round is recorded, no task that changes
      files, of role preflight, test-support (support in an earlier
      release's ledger), test, legacy, sweep, build, fix, or restore,
      closed after it in the sprint's run or the
      ledger with a path outside the test paths staged, and every live
      test has a result since that round; where no failure tests
      failures lists stands; and where each speed send-back, the latest
      verdict on its test, is settled by a tests item accepted after it
      or its test file stands at the blob the verdict recorded, its last
      passing version. A deferred product-slow verdict does not fail it.
      It prints SETTLED with the count of deferred verdicts and each
      one, or NOT SETTLED and each failure, and exits 2 where it does
      not hold.
  tests stamp --sprint <path> --commit <sha>
      After the owner commits the sprint's work and before the archive
      step moves the sprint, for a sprint under .ok-planner/sprints/ the
      closed-record ledger does not list yet: write the scenario list the
      sprint's run and its certification ledger leave, and write no
      other file. A sprint with no -plan.json reads as a plan of no
      tests, so every scenario, story stamp, unguarded program, budget,
      and kept file stands as recorded, and the stamp writes the
      ranking, the speed acceptances, and the reaches its rounds
      leave. It records each scenario whose
      test the sprint wrote or kept, with its test, test support files,
      locks, silence, waits, kind, and setup, and each gap; it carries
      the lock list, and writes test_support_locks from the scenario
      list's overlaid by the sprint's latest locks item per path, for
      each path a stamped scenario lists as test support, and refuses a
      lock name the lock list does not hold or a mode other than
      exclusive and shared;
      a planned new scenario whose test was never written gets no
      record, and a planned rewrite or retirement the sprint never
      made keeps its earlier record. Each scenario the plan does not
      name stands as recorded. A scenario failing in its standing
      result records the open issue it waits on: an owner issue, a
      held promise, or the issue on a promised ledger defect. Each
      story the plan planned whose every planned scenario has its
      test written, kept, or retired, or is a gap, gets as its test
      stamp the git blob id of .ok-planner/design/stories/<slug>.md at
      <sha>; every other story the plan planned loses its stamp. It
      writes the ranking, each test's median over the passing
      whole-suite runs at its test file's blob, the ranking's carried
      values included, charged under the stamped tests' locks with the
      budget's workers, as tests run ranks, and adds the sprint
      to ranked_sprints, the sprints whose rounds the ranking holds; a
      stamp of a sprint ranked_sprints already names ranks from the
      carried values alone, so a second stamp counts no round twice;
      each test's speed
      acceptance, the latest accepted verdict, or the carried one
      where no verdict was given, while its test file stands at the
      accepted blob; and each test's reaches, the latest run's list
      over the carried map. It also writes each inferred rule the run
      ported, {rule, check, command, test, sprint}, beside the ones
      earlier stamps recorded, the test files the plan kept, and the
      plan's budget, its preflight mark set to the id of the sprint's
      latest preflight task the plan does not set aside where it closed
      done, else kept as the plan carries it. It refuses a scenario
      list still in the earlier form, with one top-level stamp:
      convert-state converts it. The stamp reads and writes the
      scenario list under its lock.
  tests rules [--json]
      Each inferred rule a stamp recorded that no take settled and whose
      command still stands in the review loop's checks. It needs no
      testing config, and prints none where no stamp recorded one.
  tests rules take <check> --as practice:<slug> | retired
      Settle the inferred rule of one check: as the practice
      .ok-planner/practices/<slug>.md, which must exist, or retired. It
      refuses a check no stamp recorded, and one already settled.

The scenario list lives in .ok-planner/test-state.json, which only
this tool writes, each writer under one lock, .ok-planner/.cache/
tests-state.lock, reading the list inside it and replacing the file
atomically: each story's test stamp under stamps, the git blob
id of the story's file as a plan last planned it, each scenario with
its test, its test support files, what its test waits on, its kind and
setup, and its locks, the plan's budget
with its preflight mark, the id of the preflight task that last passed
against it, each program the owner chose to leave unguarded,
each inferred rule a port carries, with how it was settled, the
ranking, each test's seconds from every passing whole-suite run at
the git blob of its test file, with its median and cost, and each
test's speed acceptance, {blob, support, seconds}, support the digest
of its test support files, each test's
reaches, the product files and folders its latest run reached, the
lock list under lock_list, [{name, guards}] sorted by name, and the
locks each test support file declares under test_support_locks,
{<path>: [<lock>, ...]}.
A test declares locks, a list of {name, mode}, mode exclusive or
shared, each name one the lock list holds, no name twice; a test that
declares none is isolated. A test takes the locks of every test
support file it lists: its effective locks are the union of its own
and theirs, a name held exclusive where either declares it so. A
tests item's locks, where it records them, replace the plan's, and
its reach, entry, waits, kind, and setup replace the plan's. A locks
item, in the locks pool of a sprint's run or certification ledger,
records --field 'locks=[...]' with --field path=<test support file>,
which replaces the scenario list's locks for that file, the latest
item winning, or --field scenario=<id>, whose names join that test's
own, every such item adding. A locks item that also records
--field defect=<id>, a fixer's record of a race it traced, adds its
names to the file or test it keys: a test support task's later item
and a tests item's locks replace no name it added, and the stamp
records each name it added, on a kept test the plan does not name
too. A name joins as exclusive where any declaration holds it so.
A tests item never changes a scenario's text, its outcome. Every
verb that reads a declaration refuses a scenario list, a plan, or a
planner item in the earlier form that records resources or reads, or
a scenario list with no lock_list, naming /ok and tests convert-state.

A sprint with no -plan.json, such as one approved before sprints planned
their tests, plans no tests: run, failures, tasks, and settled read it
as a plan of no scenarios over the project's folders, with the budget
and the kept files the scenario list records; stray lists no file for
it, since it keeps every test file it leaves alone, so the sweep
deletes none and a frozen certification refuses none; plan --show,
run --changed, and stamp refuse it. A sprint's certification ledger is
the selected run where it is a /converge ledger whose sprint-review
prompt certifies the sprint, or else the ledger the completion report's
# Sprint certification section names; its tests items join the sprint
run's, the later per scenario.

Every --sprint names a sprint under .ok-planner/sprints/, and the tool
refuses any other path, an archived sprint's included.

Exit 0: done. Exit 1: usage, input, or read error. Exit 2: tests settled
finds the suite unsettled.""" % RESULT_LINE_BYTES

